          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does osteoarthritis guidance describe as core treatment before anything else?",
          "answer": "According to the osteoarthritis guidance (*Osteoarthritis: exercise-first management and the sequence of treatment options*, from a rheumatology and orthopedic society summary), the core first treatment is non-drug management, with structured exercise at its center.\n\nThe guidance says the exercise program combines:\n- Strengthening the muscles around the affected joint.\n- Range-of-movement work.\n- Aerobic activity.\n\nIt says supervision is useful at least at the start, because sticking with it and progressing correctly affect the result. The guidance also says exercise helps regardless of X-ray severity, age, or how much pain someone has at the start. It notes that pain during exercise within tolerable limits does not mean the joint is being damaged.\n\nThe retrieved passages did not cover the later steps in the treatment sequence, such as medicines or procedures, so I can't say what comes after exercise. A clinician can talk through those options and whether an exercise program suits your situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
